+# Pod 2 Demo Day feedback → what we changed
+
+Source: *Demo Day Feedback Synthesis — Mobilizing Pod 2* (15 reviewer
+responses, Ideation Demo Day, Jul 10–16; synthesis by Emmanuel, Jul 2026).
+
+Scores were Church Launch Kit 3.7 · Volunteer Connect 3.6 · Church Resource
+Hub 3.3, with the ordering holding when pod members' own ratings were excluded.
+The three cross-cutting themes were **consolidate**, **segment the persona**,
+and **design for after the yes**.
+
+This file tracks each piece of feedback to the thing that answers it, so the
+next check-in can be about what changed rather than about what we heard.
+
+## Answered in this pass
+
+| Feedback (who raised it) | What we did | Where |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| "Church" isn't a persona — which church: newly aware, willing pastor / unready congregation, or ready but directionless? (Aaron) | Built the Readiness Pathway as a four-tier self-assessment whose tier names *are* Aaron's three church types, plus an "already going" graduation state. Readiness is scored as a floor, not an average — a church sits at the earliest gate it hasn't cleared. | `src/lib/readiness.ts`, `/readiness` |
+| Too many similar ideas; pick one summer focus; lowest effort / highest impact (Aaron, Erin, Kezia, R. Dong) | The Pathway is the front door and the only new product surface. Playbook Exchange stays unbuilt pending the gap analysis; the Campus Coordination Network is explicitly deferred. Everything else this pass extends ChurchedIn rather than adding a fourth thing. | this file, `docs/ISM_IN_A_BOX_GAP_ANALYSIS.md` |
+| Volunteer Connect is undefined — platform, CRM, training, or guide? (Aaron) | Answered by shipping, as the synthesis proposed: it's a per-church coordination space with three verbs — plan a gathering, RSVP, find a friend. Now stated in those terms on the landing page. | `src/app/page.tsx` |
+| Volunteer supply and motivation; early volunteers should recruit the hesitant (Thanh, Aaron, Gardion) | Volunteer stories: a first-person account from someone who already hosted, church-scoped by default and optionally visible on the church's profile page (first name only) where a student choosing a church will see it. Plus a derived activity feed so a church space never looks abandoned, and a dashboard nudge that fires once — only for a volunteer who has actually hosted and hasn't written one. | `/stories`, `src/lib/churchActivity.ts` |
+| Migration from existing group chats / tools (R. Dong) | A landing section that leads with the three things a group chat structurally can't do — auto-waitlists, a privacy-gated friend directory, and a per-church space with a join code — framed as "keep your WhatsApp group" rather than asking anyone to switch. | `src/app/BeyondGroupChat.tsx` |
+| Never demo from an empty database (synthesis action item) | `npm run db:seed` builds a demo church: 10 people, 3 gatherings (one past, one deliberately over capacity so the waitlist is real), connections in accepted/pending/declined states, an open and a claimed ride, and two stories. Re-runnable, and it refuses to touch a church that isn't its own. | `prisma/seed.ts` |
+| Show ChurchedIn as the graduation destination for a "ready" church (synthesis action item) | Only the two ready tiers carry a ChurchedIn call-to-action; a newly-aware church is told plainly that we're not much use to it yet and pointed at the map instead. There's a unit test asserting that, so it can't drift. | `src/lib/readiness.ts`, `readiness.test.ts` |
+
+## Answered on paper, not in code
+
+| Feedback (who raised it) | Status |
+| --- | --- |
+| Differentiate from ISM-in-a-box and IFI's existing toolkit (Aaron, Thanh) | Framework and the one-sentence gap statement are written; **the empirical claims are unverified** and someone has to read the actual source material before the check-in. See `docs/ISM_IN_A_BOX_GAP_ANALYSIS.md`, which is deliberately left with its comparison table unfilled. |
+| Follow-up pipeline: what happens after a church gets the kit? (R. Dong, Danielle, Jonathan) | Partially answered: the Pathway now defines a next step at every tier, and ChurchedIn is where the step happens. The harder half — year-two sustainability, a church that hosts in September and nothing in February — is not answered by anything we've built, and the gap analysis says so rather than claiming it. |
+| Uniting churches across denominations is its own problem space (Jonathan) | Deferred, as the synthesis recommended. Per-church scoping sidesteps it for now; `ChurchPartnership` is the seam it would grow from. |
+
+## Deliberately not done
+
+- **Student-written stories.** A student's account of being welcomed is
+  probably the single most persuasive thing that could sit on a church's
+  profile page, and that is exactly why it isn't shipped: it needs a consent
+  design of its own before a student's words go anywhere a stranger can read
+  them. Story authorship is limited to volunteers and church leaders.
+- **Persisting readiness answers.** A church shouldn't need an account to find
+  out it isn't ready for one. The assessment runs client-side and stores
+  nothing, which also means we have no readiness data to analyze later — an
+  accepted trade.
+- **Broadcasting connections or rides in the activity feed.** Both are
+  one-to-one relationships behind an accept step; putting "Ana connected with
+  David" in a church-wide feed would leak precisely what the accept step
+  exists to protect.
